  {
    "episode_id": 50929,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50929.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 78,
    "turn_text": "Scared as hell? I mean, I look at what's happening. I think about, you know, my mom and dad. My mom and dad grew up during the Great Depression. My mom never graduated from high school. She left home when she was 16 because they didn't have enough money to put food on the table. So she went to Georgia and became a nanny. My dad went into World War II, fought the Japanese in New Guinea. He was the B.A.R. guy. in his platoon. Was it Marine? He was an army. And so because he had the most firepower, the Japanese snipers, that was the first guy they wanted to pick off. So my dad saw a lot of action. One of the bravest, most gutsy guys I've ever met in my life. But they left me a better America. than they got by far, right? And I can't say that right now. And I believe that the only way we're having served in the belly of the beast in Washington DC and seeing that cast of characters back there, I do not have the confidence that they're going to turn this country around. I really don't. I believe if we're going to do it, it's going to be one state at a time.",
