    "turn_text": "in capitalism, conservatives like us talk about school choice and vouchers as a way of allowing parents to vote every day, even when it's not the election cycle. And what this does is it brings politics and empowers individuals in their school systems to vote for good and bad schools with their money, which speaks, everybody's full of shit until money's involved. And you're like, oh, okay, this is real. You can tell a school board, and they can put you on a two minute timer and shut you up. You can tell a school board this, you can tell a school board that, but when you take your money, that shuts them up right in their place. And it scares them. And so I'm just getting everybody a little context because we talk about these in these 30-second news bits that we've been trained into for the last 100 years. But really, your typical Democrat or left-of-center person says, the tax dollars in your neighborhood should stay in your neighborhood and go to those schools and they should be sacrosanct. That's part of that community that you live in. and what they're doing is forcing this imposition of where the money goes and locking it up so it's theirs. And what conservatives or freedom people are thinking from the opposite of the spectrum is, well, that's great if the school's great. If the school's not great, I should be able to take my tax dollars and divert them with my kid to better education. And what happens is in the free market, the local school has to adjust because the money goes down or the local school closes. Or they go out of business. Right.",
